{
  "answer": "Overfitting is a situation in machine learning where a model performs exceptionally well on the training data but poorly on unseen data. This occurs when a model is too complex, capturing noise in the training data rather than the underlying pattern. To prevent overfitting, techniques such as regularization, dropout, and early stopping can be used. Additionally, using a simpler model or collecting more data can also help reduce overfitting.",
  "confidence": "high",
  "key_points": ["Overfitting is when a model performs well on training data but poorly on unseen data", "Complex models are prone to overfitting", "Regularization, dropout, and early stopping are techniques to prevent overfitting", "Simpler models or more data can also help prevent overfitting"]
}

{
  "answer": "The attention mechanism in transformers is a key component that allows the model to focus on different parts of the input sequence during the computation of the final output. It is used to compute the weighted sum of the input sequence, which is essential for understanding the context of each token in the sequence. The attention mechanism works by computing a score between each token in the input sequence and each token in the context, and then normalizing these scores to produce a distribution over the context tokens. This distribution is then used to weight the corresponding tokens in the input sequence, effectively allowing the model to give more importance to certain parts of the input sequence based on their relevance to the current token being processed.",
  "confidence": "high",
  "key_points": ["attention mechanism", "computes weighted sum of input sequence", "focuses on different parts of the input sequence", "used in transformers", "computes scores between tokens", "normalizes scores", "produces distribution over context tokens", "weights input sequence based on relevance"]
}

{
  "answer": "Precision and recall are two important metrics used in evaluating the performance of a binary classifier. Precision is the ratio of correctly predicted positive instances to the total predicted positive instances. It measures the accuracy of positive predictions. Recall, on the other hand, is the ratio of correctly predicted positive instances to the total actual positive instances. It measures the ability of the classifier to find all the positive instances. In other words, precision is concerned with the accuracy of the positive predictions, while recall is concerned with the completeness of the positive predictions.",
  "confidence": "high",
  "key_points": ["Precision measures the accuracy of positive predictions", "Recall measures the ability to find all positive instances"]
}
